<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagram</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bagram is a town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in Parwan province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, near the meeting of the Ghorband and Panjshir rivers. It lies near the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Kapisa</a>, where excavations in the 1930s uncovered the Begram hoard of Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a>, Roman glass and Chinese lacquer. In modern times it was the site of a major air base, used by the Soviet army in the 1980s and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces from 2001 to 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagram.jpg" alt="Bagram" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JE at English Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagrambazaar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indo-Greek Kingdom was never one state but a number of Hellenistic kingdoms ruled from regional capitals such as Taxila, Sagala, Pushkalavati and <b>Bagram</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They ruled from Kapisa, near modern <b>Bagram</b>, and the Kabul valley over a population that was largely Buddhist and Hindu.</blockquote><a href="/state/nezak-huns">Nezak Huns</a></li></ul></section>
